{"joint_training_upperbound": "Joint Training (Upperbound) refers to a method or approach used as an upper limit or benchmark in evaluating performance, specifically in the context of machine learning or data analysis tasks.|Here is another description: Joint Training (Upperbound) refers to a method or approach used as an upper limit or benchmark in evaluating performance, specifically in the context of transfer learning where multiple datasets or tasks are involved."},
{"joint_training_upperbound__has_forward_transfer__6_97": "The predicate \"has_forward_transfer\" indicates the presence or measurement of forward transfer, which is a concept in machine learning where knowledge or performance gains from one task are applied to improve performance in a subsequent task."},
{"6_97": "The object \"6.97\" represents a numerical value, likely indicating the degree or amount of forward transfer observed in the Joint Training (Upperbound) method, measured in a specific unit relevant to the context, such as percentage points or another performance metric.|Here is another description: The score of 6.97 represents the highest performance level achieved by joint training in forward transfer tasks. This score serves as a benchmark to evaluate the effectiveness of other training strategies, such as Gauss-Tin, which aim to approach this level of performance while operating under more practical constraints, such as sequential task arrival."},
{"joint_training_upperbound__has_backward_transfer__19_57": "The predicate \"has_backward_transfer\" indicates the presence or measurement of backward transfer in the context of machine learning or transfer learning. Backward transfer refers to the impact or effect that learning a new task has on the performance of previously learned tasks."},
